  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Madhav Mandal And 2 Others vs State Of U.P. on 2 January, 2024 High Court Of Judicature At Allahabad ?Neutral Citation No. - 2024:Ahc:136 Court No. - 84 Case :- Criminal Appeal No. - 4361 of 2022 Appellant :- Madhav Mandal And 2 Others Respondent :- State of U.P. Counsel for Appellant :- Anand Kumar Singh,Roshni Murad,Suraj Kumar Singh",
    "facts": "1. This Criminal appeal under Section 374 (2) of the Criminal Procedure Code has been preferred by the appellants Madhav Mandal, Taposh Sikandar & Sapan Vishwas against the conviction and sentence recorded vide judgment and order dated 30.05.2022 passed by the Additional District and Sessions Judge, Court No.5, Varanasi in Sessions Trial No.508 of 2021, arising out of case crime no.53 of 2021, under Sections 188, 273 I.P.C. and Section 31(1)/63 Food Safety Standard Act, P.S. Lanka, District Varanasi wherein the maximum punishment was imposed upon the appellants under Section 273 I.P.C. for imprisonment of ten years. By means of the bail application, the appellants-applicants seeks suspension of sentence and Madhav Mandal And 2 Others vs State Of U.P. on 2 January, 2024 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/46118806/ 1 grant of bail.",
    "arguments": "It has been further submitted that the conviction and sentence imposed by the learned trial Judge is against the weight of evidence on record. The trial court has misread the evidence on record and convicted the appellants. The prosecution has not been able to prove its case beyond reasonable doubt. It has been further submitted that the appellants were on bail during trial and have not misused the liberty of bail. Since there is no likelihood of early hearing of the appeal in near future, the appellants may be released on bail pending appeal. 5. Learned counsel for the appellants has further submitted that the impugned judgment and order has been passed without appreciating the material evidence on record."
  },
